my name is Delaney Baker I am a musician  in Nashville this is my wife Tommy and  this is our story towards debt freedom  we got married fairly young I was right  out of college and to say we were broke  would be an exaggeration we had no money  what happened three years and three  months ago that inspired this big change  in your life to get out of that we both  had separate checking accounts I didn't  know what he was bringing in I didn't  know how much money we made I had no  clue she was working in a salon about  the time that I was playing in a cover  band  that was doing really well I always had  cash in my pocket we went into that  summer and it was like okay that's fine  you know and I didn't watch where it  went and so it just slipped through my  fingers like water the winter months are  slow for a musician gigs kind of slow  down Christmas break take some of my  students so it was December of 2011 when  I realized that I didn't have money to  pay rent in January and I don't think  the stress that he was feeling even  registered to me because every time  money was tight he would always find  work it didn't click to me that we were  in trouble she didn't know because we  weren't talking about it I was so  emotionally wrecked that I brought it  all the God got down on my knees ugly  cry pull your heart out prayer and it  was we need to do this better we can do  this better and I don't know what that  looks like yet but I'm open to learning  I hadn't felt the weight and felt the  pain of the debt until that December  you're like okay god I need some help  here that's right and he looks over at  Gabriel goes hey look  and woke up so I reached out to a friend  of mine David Stevenson and asked him  for his help  sitting down and doing my taxes Davis  said hey look we're actually starting an  FPU class and we would like for you guys  to attend we got a plan of action  together it's it's so much fun I started  really meal planning and couponing and  making laundry so it was exciting to  know that we could work together a  common goal so we were a year and a half  into the plan  $56,000 when we started so we had paid  off twenty five thousand dollars at this  point we were living in a three-bedroom  house with a huge backyard paying rent  to my parents who owned the place and we  get a call saying your dad's job may be  in jeopardy we have to sell the house I  wonder by  my heart wanted to buy it just because  we'd made it home it was a heartbreaking  yeah  but on the other hand and we're still  30-some thousand dollars in debt if we  moved into much smaller apartment and it  was like a smack in the face it was  reality setting in this is what it means  to be living within our means we realize  we are very spoiled you've paid off  fifty six thousand dollars yeah one of  the secrets to getting out of debt  communication sitting down and doing the  budget we did it weekly yes we did when  we attended FPU we realized the  importance of sitting down and doing a  budget weekly and making sure that we  launched every penny Thursdays or our  days off and we get up and make coffee  and make a big breakfast we'll play  cards and then we'll sit down and get to  business it became one of the sweetest  times of the week just because we were  living with an irregular income we can  no longer use that as an excuse what we  gave up in order to maintain our heading  we look back at now and I'm grateful it  was a temporary pain I never got a  chance to take my wife on a honeymoon  and so we just save up hasn't always  been easy and there been many dissonant  moments where we felt discouraged and  defeated  but looking back those times were  essential to creating a deeper  appreciation just how sweet and  meaningful this verse of our lives has  been you can't wait to start the next
 

